Question 2:

How do we write to an LCD Display?
Adding LCD to Your Servo Code
The LCD code slots right in alongside your existing movement code. Nothing else changes.
#include <Wire.h>
#include <hd44780.h>
#include <hd44780ioClass/hd44780_I2Cexp.h>

hd44780_I2Cexp lcd;
// After writing the servo position:
lcd.clear();
lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
lcd.print("Servo at:");
lcd.print(pos[0]);
The new lcd. lines just print after each servo move. The servo logic stays untouched.
lcd.begin(16, 2);
lcd.backlight();
Let’s start building our robotic arm!
Start by attaching the control box to the platform using four nylon screws/nuts.

The built-in standoffs should be facing up.
Step 1
Attach the rubber feet to the underside as shown here.

Do not cover any of the mounting holes.
Step 1b
Install the breakout board with the attached nano into the control box using four nylon screws and nuts.
The ports on the board have corresponding openings in the control box.
Step 2
Install the LCD display into the control panel using four nylon screws/nuts.
Note the orientation in the picture shown.
Step 3
Separate a bundle of four wires from the jumpers.
Keep the four wires together
Connect the I2C pins on the display to the I2C pins on the breakout board: 
Gnd -> Gnd
Vcc -> 5V
SDA -> SDA
SCL -> SCL
Step 4
Connect all five servos to the digital/servo pins (2 -> 6) on the breakout board. 
Brown connects to ground, orange connects to signal.
Now connect your USB cable and upload the Arduino code. 
This will initialize all of the servos to a known good position (90 degrees)
As we build the arm this ensures we get 0-180 degrees of motion from all of them.
Step 5
After the initialization step, you will see this message (or similar).
This is OK, we have not installed the joysticks yet.
Step 5b
Using the sharp/long screws that come with the servos, mount a servo into the base of the arm.
The output gear of the servo should be in the center of the base.
Step 6
Mount the base of the arm to the platform using four nylon screws/nuts.  Use the channels provided to run the wires to the control box.
Note the orientation, with the open portion facing the control box.
This is the rotation servo. It connects to digital pin 6.
Step 7
Insert a servo horn into the rotating platform piece (note the slots where it fits), and use one of the machine screws to attach it to the servo.
This screw should go in very easily - don’t use a lot of force, that could strip the threads.
Position this piece as shown in the photo, with the open areas aligned.
Step 8
Use this type of servo horn.
The protruding center points down and fits into the opening in this piece.
Pull the caps off of the joysticks, and mount them to the small panels using four nylon screws/nuts. 
They fit in one orientation only.
Step 9
Push the caps back on.
They are keyed to a specific orientation – rotate them until they fit comfortably.
Step 10
Separate a bundle of five jumper wires and connect them to the first joystick.
For the other three joysticks, use a single wire on the leftmost pin, and a bundle of three wires on the three rightmost.
Pins from left to right in this orientation:
Button | Y | X | VCC | GND.
Step 11
Start mounting the joysticks and connecting them to the breakout board as shown:
Joystick 1 X pin       	A6
Joystick 1 Y pin       	A7
Joystick 1 Button pin  	7
Joystick 2 X pin       	A1
Joystick 2 Button pin  	8
Joystick 3 X pin       	A2
Joystick 3 Button pin  	9
Joystick 4 X pin       	A0
Joystick 4 Button pin 	10
Leftmost is Joy1, rightmost is Joy4.
The Vcc/Gnd connections on the joystick can go to any vcc/gnd pins on the board. 
Align them to either the analog or digital pin connection.
Step 12
Button | Y | X | VCC | GND.
Use the “S” pins for the signal connections.
